" wk's system vimrc (installed at $VIM/vimrc by the container build).
" The terminal is alacritty-backed and speaks 256-colour SGR; vim's builtin
" termcap can't know that, so declare it — defaults.vim then turns syntax
" highlighting on.
set t_Co=256

" That builtin termcap also leaves the ANSI colour-set entries (t_AF/t_AB)
" empty, so vim can't emit ANY foreground/background colour — syntax
" highlighting and the Visual selection would show nothing. Declare the
" standard xterm-256colour sequences (\e = ESC); the Visual group's ctermbg
" then renders, as do syntax colours.
let &t_AF = "\e[38;5;%dm"
let &t_AB = "\e[48;5;%dm"

" Write files in place instead of vim's default rename-based save. A wk file
" node is a single-file mount (like a Docker bind-mount of one file): the
" default save writes a fresh file and renames it over the original, which
" severs the mount — the new bytes land in a throwaway vfs file and never reach
" the backing file (or any node sharing it). This is the exact Docker
" single-file bind-mount gotcha, and the same fix applies: backupcopy=yes
" truncates and rewrites the original in place, so edits persist and propagate;
" nowritebackup skips the intermediate backup entirely.
set backupcopy=yes
set nowritebackup

" wk's vfs reports no POSIX permission bits (wasi has none), so vim flags every
" file [readonly] via its `!(perm & 0222)` check. Every file here is in fact
" writable (even image-layer files, which copy-up on first write), so clear the
" spurious flag; a genuinely unwritable backing file still fails at :w time.
autocmd BufReadPost,BufNewFile * setlocal noreadonly
